{
  "@context": {
    "schema": "https://schema.org/"
  },
  "@type": "Note",
  "@id": "note:notion-437",
  "title": "Glossary",
  "author": "person:jane-brew:001",
  "status": "draft",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "b-1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Glossary"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-3",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Spline**"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-4",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "---"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-5",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In the context of graphics, a spline is a type of curve used in computer-aided design (CAD) and computer graphics. A spline is a piecewise function that defines a"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-6",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "smooth, continuous curve between two or more control points."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-7",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In graphics, splines are often used to create smooth, natural-looking shapes and surfaces, such as:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-8",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1. Curves: Splines can be used to draw curved lines and shapes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-9",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "2. Surfaces: Splines can be used to create 3D models of complex surfaces, such as mountains or buildings."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-10",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "3. Animation: Splines are often used in animation to define the movement of objects over time."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-11",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Splines are typically defined by a set of control points, also known as knots, which specify the shape and orientation of the curve or surface. The spline is then"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-12",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "calculated using a mathematical formula that interpolates between these control points."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-13",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "There are several types of splines used in graphics, including:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-14",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1. Bezier curves: These curves use a set of control points to define the shape of the curve."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-15",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "2. Catmull-Rom splines: These splines are a type of Bézier curve that provide a smooth, natural-looking fit between control points."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-16",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "3. NURBS (Non-Uniform Rational B-spline): This is a more advanced type of spline that provides greater accuracy and control."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-17",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Splines have several advantages in graphics, including:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-18",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1. Smooth curves: Splines can create smooth, continuous curves that are difficult to achieve with other methods."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-19",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "2. Flexibility: Splines can be used to define complex shapes and surfaces."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-20",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "3. Accuracy: Splines provide a high degree of accuracy and precision."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-21",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "However, splines also have some limitations, including:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-22",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1. Computational complexity: Calculating splines can be computationally intensive."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-23",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "2. Control point manipulation: Manipulating control points to change the shape of the spline can be challenging."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-24",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Overall, splines are an essential tool in graphics and computer-aided design, providing a powerful means of creating smooth, natural-looking curves and surfaces."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-26",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-25",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Tweening**"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-27",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "---"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-28",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Tweening is a visual effect technique used in animation, filmmaking, and video editing to create the illusion of smooth motion between two or more keyframes. It"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-29",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "involves interpolating (i.e., estimating) intermediate values between the start and end points of an object's movement or other graphical elements over time."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-30",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In traditional hand-drawn animation, tweening was a manual process where animators would draw each frame in between the keyframes to create a smooth motion. With"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-31",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "the advent of digital technology, tweening became more efficient and accessible through computer software."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-32",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "There are different types of tweening:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-33",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "1. **Linear interpolation**: This is the simplest type of tweening, where the intermediate values are calculated by taking a straight line between the start and end"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-34",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "points."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-35",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "2. **Bezier interpolation**: This type of tweening uses Bezier curves to create a smoother, more natural motion."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-36",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "3. **Tweener**: A tweener is a specialized plugin or software that automates the tweening process, allowing users to easily create smooth motion between keyframes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-37",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Tweening can be used for various purposes, such as:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-38",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Creating realistic character movements"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-39",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Animating special effects (e.g., explosions, fire)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-40",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Transitions between scenes"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-41",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- Enhancing video editing"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-42",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "However, excessive or poorly executed tweening can lead to distracting visual effects that detract from the overall animation. As a result, many animators and"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-43",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "filmmakers strive for a balance between smooth motion and subtle visual cues."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-44",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "-"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-46",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-45",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Kerning**"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-47",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "-"
        }
